WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.245
A dashboard helps only while someone is looking at it, and at 3 a.m., nobody is.

2
00:00:08.245 --> 00:00:11.225
So Prometheus checks the conditions itself.

3
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:16.313
An alerting rule is a PromQL query with a name, like HelloDown.

4
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:21.383
The comparison filters, so the query returns something only when a scrape fails.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.078
Prometheus runs it every 15 seconds in the lab.

6
00:00:25.078 --> 00:00:28.333
Every series it returns is one active alert.

7
00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:31.909
Its label, severity page, is added to the alert.

8
00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:33.999
Alertmanager will route on it.

9
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.516
An alert moves through three states: inactive, pending, firing.

10
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:40.614
Two checks pass.

11
00:00:40.614 --> 00:00:42.376
The alert is inactive.

12
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.940
Then a scrape fails.

13
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.347
The alert is pending.

14
00:00:45.347 --> 00:00:48.764
for: 1m means it must stay true for a whole minute.

15
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:53.517
If one check is false, it goes back to inactive, and the wait starts again.

16
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:57.900
So for: filters out blips, like one slow scrape during a deploy.

17
00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.895
Now the scrapes keep failing.

18
00:00:59.895 --> 00:01:03.843
Pending again, and after a whole minute of true checks, it fires.

19
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.415
But Prometheus only decides that an alert fires.

20
00:01:08.415 --> 00:01:10.355
It sends no message itself.

21
00:01:10.355 --> 00:01:13.157
It hands firing alerts to Alertmanager.

22
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:21.220
One broken database can trip dozens of rules at once, and each firing alert is re-sent about once a minute.

23
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.624
Straight to a phone, that is a flood.

24
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.770
So Alertmanager groups them.

25
00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.989
Alerts with the same group_by labels become one notification.

26
00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:33.240
Fifty pods failing give one message, not fifty.

27
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:37.505
Then each group walks down a tree of routes, matched on labels.

28
00:01:37.505 --> 00:01:42.120
In the lab, severity page goes to pager, and everything else to tickets.

29
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:45.690
Inhibition: while one alert fires, it mutes others.

30
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.560
The lab mutes ticket alerts for a job while a page fires for the same job.

31
00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:56.040
Silences are temporary mutes you add at run time, with amtool or in the UI.

32
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.289
They are not in the config file.

33
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.476
Now a real outage.

34
00:02:00.476 --> 00:02:04.163
Stop Redis, and every request to hello-devops fails.

35
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:10.880
Soon both error budget alerts are pending: the fast burn, a page, and the slow burn, a ticket.

36
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.048
Two minutes later, both fire.

37
00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:19.004
Alertmanager waits 10 seconds, the group_wait, for more alerts.

38
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:21.351
Then inhibition.

39
00:02:21.351 --> 00:02:27.009
The page fires for the job hello-devops, so the ticket for the same job is muted.

40
00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:31.829
The page walks down the tree to the pager, and the webhook prints one line.

41
00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:33.177
Only the page arrived.

42
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.777
You are working on it, so you silence the page for an hour.

43
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.977
Fix Redis, then expire the silence, so you will hear when the alert resolves.

44
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.898
The long windows still hold the outage.

45
00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:48.357
About 25 minutes after the fix, the page resolved.

46
00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:53.383
In the same second, Alertmanager released the ticket it had been muting.

47
00:02:53.383 --> 00:02:55.529
The outage really did spend budget.

48
00:02:57.033 --> 00:02:59.747
Prometheus decides that an alert fires.

49
00:02:59.747 --> 00:03:02.529
Alertmanager decides who hears about it.
